// Time.
//
// `now()` and `sleep(ms)` are builtins, in scope in every file, so this module
// documents the names rather than defining them.
//
// `now()` answers milliseconds since the Unix epoch as an `int`. It reads the
// wall clock, not a monotonic one: a system clock adjustment can make a later
// call return a smaller number. Use it for timestamps and for coarse elapsed
// measurements, not for ordering that must never go backwards.
//
// `sleep(ms)` blocks for at least `ms` milliseconds and returns unit. A value
// of zero or less is a no-op; it does not yield to the scheduler on its own.
// The wait is interruptible on POSIX and is retried after a signal.
//
//     let start = now();
//     sleep(20);
//     let elapsed = now() - start;
//     print("waited at least 20 ms: ");
//     print(str_from_i64(elapsed));
//     print("\n");
//
// Ownership: neither creates nor consumes a value.
